<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas François Burgers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1834–1881</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas François Burgers was a Cape-born minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-reformed-church">Dutch Reformed Church</a> who served as president of the South African Republic from 1872 to 1877. He promoted a railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a>, secular state schooling and the republic's first gold coins, but his plans outran its revenue, and a failed war against the Pedi king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sekhukhune-i">Sekhukhune</a> in 1876 left the state bankrupt, opening the way to the British annexation of 1877.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-francois-burgers.jpg" alt="Thomas François Burgers" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Offenberg Photographie, Damstraat, Amsterdam · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ThFBurgers_CHM_VA0897_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
